 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Laughing at how movies stuff product placement into every scene, Max, Fedya, and Senya hold up a bottle of cooking oil like actors in a commercial before debating their favorite film genres.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Федя, дай мне масло.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Пожалуйста, вот твоё масло.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "О, это моё любимое масло!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ну да, всё правильно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Потом в кинотеатре мы плюёмся.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "А потом снимают вторую часть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Арсений у нас настоящий кинокритик.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "А сам ты что смотришь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вот молоток уже освободился.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Спасибо, мне уже не нужен.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Я люблю хорошее мужское кино.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ненавижу эти скучные понедельники.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's walk through the product-placement parody line by line.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Федя, дай мне масло.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Fedya, give me the oil.' The Russian word for 'butter' and 'cooking oil' is the exact same neuter noun.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Пожалуйста, вот твоё масло.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Here you go, here is your oil.' When handing someone an object, the word for 'please' means 'here you go'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "О, это моё любимое масло!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh, this is my favorite oil!' Notice how Russian puts 'favorite' and 'butter' together in a short, direct phrase where word endings show how the ideas connect.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ну да, всё правильно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well yes, that is exactly right.' Senya nods at how accurately they just imitated a movie commercial.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Потом в кинотеатре мы плюёмся.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Then at the movie theater we spit in disgust.' In everyday Russian, 'later, afterwards' flows straight into 'disgust' without extra linking words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "А потом снимают вторую часть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And then they film a second part.' Senya pairs 'later, afterwards' with 'part, share; unit' directly, since spoken Russian skips English filler words like 'the', 'a', and 'is'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Арсений у нас настоящий кинокритик.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Our Arseny is a real film critic.' Compound nouns with 'kino-' refer to cinema, here 'film critic'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "А сам ты что смотришь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what do you watch yourself?' The emphatic pronoun 'yourself' challenges the critic to name his own taste.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вот молоток уже освободился.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Here, the meat mallet is free now.' In everyday Russian, 'mallet' flows straight into 'to become free, to be released' without extra linking words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Спасибо, мне уже не нужен.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thanks, I do not need it anymore.' Combining 'already' with 'not needed' is how Russian says 'no longer needed'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Я люблю хорошее мужское кино.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I love a good manly movie.' The neuter noun 'cinema/movie' never changes its ending for case or number.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ненавижу эти скучные понедельники.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I hate these boring Mondays.' Senya launches his action-movie daydream with a gritty hero one-liner. Now listen to the whole exchange again at natural speed.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Федя, дай мне масло.",
   "gap": 0.5
